 {"language": {"code": "yi", "name": "Yiddish"}, "word": "פּינגווין"}, {"language": {"code": "yo", "name": "Yoruba"}, "word": "pẹ́ńgúìnì"}, {"language": {"code": "zza", "name": "Zazaki"}, "word": "penguen (diq)"}, {"language": {"code": "za", "name": "Zhuang"}, "word": "gingoz"}, {"language": {"code": "zu", "name": "Zulu"}, "word": "iphengwini"}], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(obsolete or historical) An auk (sometimes especially a great auk), a bird of the Northern Hemisphere.", "tags": ["historical", "obsolete"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "This last species of penguin, or auk, seems to be the same with the alca cirrhata of Dr. Pallis, Spicileg. Zool. Fasc. v. p. 7. tab. i. & v. fig. 1–3. F.", "reference": "1772 March, “Account of the Settlement of the Malouines”, in The Gentleman's and London Magazine, page 166:"}, {"text": "More than a hundred years ago, for example, was seen the last of the great wingless penguins or auks, which early writers quaintly called \" wobble-birds.\"", "reference": "1885, Journal of the American Geographical Society of New York:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(slang) A nun.", "tags": ["slang"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Jake (John Belushi): What are we doing here?\n Elwood (Dan Aykroyd): You promised you'd visit the penguin the day you got out.\n Jake: Yeah. So, I lied to her.\n Elwood:You can't lie to a nun. We gotta go in and visit the penguin.", "reference": "1980, Dan Aykroyd, The Blues Brothers (motion picture):"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(juggling) A type of catch where the palm of the hand is facing towards the leg with the arm stretched downward, resembling the flipper of a penguin.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "A spiny bromeliad with egg-shaped fleshy fruit, Bromelia pinguin.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "These productive patches, and the houses, were each surrounded by a fence, made of a prickly shrub, called the Pinguin, which propagates itself with great rapidity.", "reference": "1803, Robert Charles Dallas, The History of the Maroons, London: Longman and Rees, Volume 1, Letter 4, p. 82:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(UK, military, slang) A member of the air force who does not fly aircraft.", "tags": ["UK", "slang"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Although it is a permanent R.A.F. station (pre-war, that is), we meander about, even in the Mess, in battle dress and flying boots, sweaters, etc., much to the disgust of some of the more dignified 'penguins' […]", "reference": "1942, Hermann Hagedorn, Sunward I've Climbed, page 143:"}, {"text": "Notwithstanding his elevated status as the wing’s Senior Intelligence Officer, Monty, too, was a “penguin,” equal in that respect to an armament assistant, airframe mechanic, pigeon loftsman or any of the seventy-odd ground trades listed by the Royal Canadian Air Force at the war’s end.", "reference": "1994, Monty Berger, Invasions without tears: the story of Canada's top-scoring Spitfire wing in Europe during the Second World War, →ISBN, →LCCN, page xi:"}, {"text": "She may have been a “penguin”—an Air Force service member who didn't fly—but it sure beat being at home, as Brady had pointed out.", "reference": "2015 June 1, Kate Christie, In the Company of Women, Bella Books, →ISBN, →OCLC:"}, {"text": "This was to be my home for three months where ground officer cadets, referred to colloquially as ‘penguins’ were trained.", "reference": "2019 November 5, Derek Esp, An Adventure in Education: The Autobiography of an Accidental Career in Education, Troubador Publishing Ltd, →ISBN, →OCLC, page 46:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}], "source": {"url": "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/penguin", "license": {"name": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"}}}}